Subject: [PATCH] [wasmfs] Cut blocking OPFS round trips, and instrument them

Every read/write/getSize/flush in the OPFS backend is a BLOCKING cross-thread round trip: the OPFS worker sits in
emscripten_exit_with_live_runtime(), so the calling thread enqueues work, waits for that worker to be woken
(postMessage relayed via the main thread, or the heartbeat poll), and blocks until it completes. None of this is
visible in a CPU profile -- task-clock samples only on-CPU time and a waiting thread is off-CPU -- so it presented
only as a 3x spread in Minecraft world-load wall time.

Instrumenting one full run gave, reproducibly to within 4 calls across four runs:

  reads = 98304   readMB = 110.7   (~1.15 KB average)   getSize = 17660   writes = 13

~116,000 blocking round trips to move 110 MB. This patch does three things:

1. getSize/flush in library_wasmfs_opfs.js stop being `async`. FileSystemSyncAccessHandle.getSize() and .flush()
   are synchronous by spec -- that is what "Sync" in the name means -- so awaiting them bought nothing and cost
   an extra microtask turn on the OPFS worker, during which the requesting thread stays blocked. read/write were
   already sync under PTHREADS; this makes the other two consistent.

2. OPFSFile memoises getSize(), dropping the memo on write/setSize/close. Safe because WASMFS serialises per-file
   access (File holds a std::recursive_mutex, all access goes through File::locked()) and this backend is the only
   writer. This alone took getSize from 17,660 calls to 48.

3. The WasmFS proxy heartbeat drops from 50ms to 2ms. It is the FALLBACK delivery path for proxied work, so
   whenever the postMessage relay is late the requesting thread waits up to a full interval; at 50ms a few
   hundred operations is already tens of seconds. Upstream's own comment calls the relay a workaround pending
   direct worker-to-worker postMessage or Atomics.waitAsync.

Read op counters are kept in the JS (OPFS_STATS, logged every 8192 reads). They are how the above was measured
rather than guessed, and the cost of an increment against a blocking round trip is nil.

NOT included, deliberately: a 64 KB read-ahead block cache in OPFSFile. It was implemented and measured, and the
hit rate was ~zero -- proxied read count unchanged at 16,384 for the same point in the run while bytes moved went
37 MB -> 1,035 MB, i.e. a full block refetched per logical read. World-load wall time got worse (39.9/40.0/96.0/
33.7s vs 29.4/30.2/35.2/40.6s). These callers are not sequential-within-file at that granularity. A note in
read() records this so it is not re-derived.
---
--- a/src/library_wasmfs.js
+++ b/src/library_wasmfs.js
@@ -618,7 +618,17 @@
         } else {
           _emscripten_proxy_execute_queue(queue);
         }
-      }, 50);
+      // 2ms, not 50ms. This interval is the FALLBACK delivery path for proxied WasmFS work: the OPFS worker sits
+      // in emscripten_exit_with_live_runtime(), so queued work reaches it either by a postMessage relayed via
+      // the main thread or by this poll. Whenever the relay is late, the thread that asked for a file operation
+      // blocks for up to a full interval -- and WasmFS proxies EVERY read, write, getSize and flush, so at 50ms
+      // a few hundred operations is already tens of seconds. Upstream's own comment above calls the relay a
+      // workaround pending direct worker-to-worker postMessage or Atomics.waitAsync.
+      //
+      // The cost is wakeups on an otherwise idle worker; 2ms clears the browser timer floor and cuts worst-case
+      // latency 25x. If idle CPU becomes a problem the right answer is to stop proxying per operation (cache
+      // reads and sizes in opfs_backend.cpp), not to raise this back up.
+      }, 2);
   },
 
   _wasmfs_stdin_get_char__deps: ['$FS_stdin_getChar'],
